Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```js
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  let adjectiveIndex = -1;

  // Find the index of the first adjective
  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i];
    if (word === "beautiful" || word === "green") {
      adjectiveIndex = i;
      break;
    }
  }

  // Swap the positions of the first and second adjectives
  if (adjectiveIndex !== -1 && adjectiveIndex > 0) {
    [words[adjectiveIndex], words[adjectiveIndex - 1]] = [words[adjecticeIndex - 1], words[adjectiveIndex]];
  }

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  return words.join(" ");
}

const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park.";
const newSentence = switchAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(newSentence);
// Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park."
```

The function takes a sentence as input, splits it into an array of words, finds the indices of the first two adjectives ("beautiful" and "green"), swaps their positions if they exist and are not adjacent, then joins the words back into a sentence and returns it.

Feel free to modify the function to handle more complex cases or additional adjectives by adjusting the `if` statement inside the loop.

